REGIONAL REGULATION OF CIREBON REGENCY

NUMBER 1 OF 2018

CONCERNING

PROTECTION, EMPOWERMENT OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

THE REGENT OF CIREBON,

Considering:

a. that children are a trust and gift from Almighty God in whom inherent dignity and worth as complete human beings, and constitute the next generation of the nation's ideals and struggle, therefore they need to receive protection and the widest possible opportunity for survival, growth and development in a proper manner;
b. that in connection with the many women and children who need protection from various forms of violence, maltreatment, exploitation, and neglect, it is deemed necessary to have strategic efforts from the Regional Government and other parties who are obligated to provide protection to the said women and children;
c. that discrimination and violence against women and children constitute violations of human rights and crimes against humanity, therefore in order to fulfill the constitutional rights of women and children as referred to in letter b, the Regional Government may carry out coordinated functions in an integrated manner regarding the implementation of its duties, authorities, and responsibilities;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Protection, Empowerment of Women and Children.

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 14 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Regions Within the Province of West Java (State Gazette dated August 8, 1950) as amended by Law Number 4 of 1968 concerning the Establishment of Purwakarta Regency and Subang Regency by amending Law Number 14 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Regions Within the Province of West Java (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1968 Number 3, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 2851);
3. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235), as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297);
4. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
5. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
6. Law Number 36 of 2009 concerning Health (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 144, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5063);
7. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 224, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
8. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
9. Presidential Regulation Number 69 of 2008 concerning the Task Force for Prevention and Handling of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons;
10. Regulation of the Minister of Social Affairs Number 102/HUK/2007 concerning the Establishment and Operation of Shelters and Trauma Centers;
11. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 02 of 2008 concerning the Implementation of Women's Protection;
12. Regulation of the Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 03 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Child Protection;
13. Regulation of the Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 1 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards in the Field of Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
14. Regional Regulation of Cirebon Regency Number 9 of 2016 concerning the Determination of Concurrent Government Affairs that are the Authority of the Cirebon Regency Government (Regional Gazette of Cirebon Regency of 2016 Number 9, Series D.6);
15. Regional Regulation of Cirebon Regency Number 10 of 2016 concerning Procedures for the Formulation of Regional Regulations (Regional Gazette of Cirebon Regency of 2016 Number 10, Series E.3);
16. Regional Regulation of Cirebon Regency Number 12 of 2016 concerning the Establishment and Composition of Regional Apparatus of Cirebon Regency (Regional Gazette of Cirebon Regency of 2016 Number 12, Series D.7).

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF CIREBON REGENCY

and

THE REGENT OF CIREBON

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING PROTECTION, EMPOWERMENT OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN.

CHAPTER I

GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1

In this Regional Regulation, the following terms shall mean:

1. Region is Cirebon Regency;
2. Regional Government is the Regent and Regional Apparatus of Cirebon Regency as elements of the administration of Cirebon Regency government;
3. Regional House of Representatives of Cirebon Regency, hereinafter referred to as DPRD, is the regional people's representative institution as an element of regional government administration;
4. Regent is the Regent of Cirebon Regency;
5. Regional Apparatus Work Unit, hereinafter abbreviated as SKPD, is the Apparatus of Cirebon Regency;
6. Discrimination is any distinction, harassment or exclusion, whether direct or indirect, based on human differences on the basis of religion, ethnicity, race, ethnic group, social status, economic status, gender, language, political beliefs that result in the reduction, deviation or elimination of recognition, implementation or use of human rights and basic freedoms in life, whether individual or collective, in the political, economic, legal, social, cultural and other aspects of life;
7. Violence is any act unlawfully with or without means against physical and psychological that causes danger to life, body or results in the deprivation of a person's freedom;
8. Violence against women is any act based on gender differences that results in or may result in suffering or hardship to women physically, sexually or psychologically, economic or social exploitation, including threats of certain actions, coercion or arbitrary deprivation of freedom, whether occurring in the public sphere or in private life;
9. Violence against children is any act against a child that results in suffering or hardship physically, mentally, sexually, psychologically, economic or social exploitation, including neglect and ill-treatment that threatens bodily integrity and degrades the dignity of the child;
10. Domestic violence is any act against a woman and child that results in suffering or hardship physically, sexually, psychologically, economically and/or domestic neglect including threats to commit acts of coercion or unlawful deprivation of freedom within the domestic sphere;
11. Trafficking in Persons is the act of recruitment, transportation, harboring, sending, transfer, or receipt of a person by threat of violence, use of violence, abduction, confinement, forgery, fraud and abuse of power or position of vulnerability, debt bondage or giving payment or benefits, so as to obtain consent from the person holding control over that other person, whether conducted within the country or between countries, for the purpose of exploitation or resulting in the person being exploited;
12. Child is a person who has not reached the age of 18 (eighteen) years including a child still in the womb;
13. Child marriage is a marriage conducted through civil, religious or customary law with or without registration or official consent where one or both partners are children under the age of 18 years;
14. Woman is a person (human being) who has a vagina, can menstruate, become pregnant, give birth to children, and breastfeed;
15. Victim of violence is an individual or group of people who experience physical, mental or emotional suffering, economic loss, or experience neglect, reduction or deprivation of their basic rights, as a result of human rights (HAM) violations, or criminal acts as well as acts of violence committed either by state apparatus or by the state or regional government apparatus or by individuals, including the victim's heirs;
16. Protection is a series of fulfillment of the rights of women and child victims of violence and discrimination starting from prevention, handling and empowerment efforts;
17. Prevention is a direct effort made by the Regional Government to prevent acts of violence against women and children and Indirect;
18. Integrated Service Center for Women and Children Empowerment, hereinafter abbreviated as P2TP2A, is an Integrated Service Unit established by the Regional Government to provide services to women and child victims of violence comprehensively including information services, assistance and legal aid, counseling services, medical services and safe houses through referral;
19. Task Force for Prevention and Handling of Trafficking in Persons Crimes, hereinafter referred to as the Task Force, is a coordinating institution tasked with coordinating prevention and handling efforts for trafficking in persons crimes in Cirebon Regency;
20. Parents are biological father and/or mother, or step father and/or mother, or adoptive father and/or mother;
21. Family is a person who has a blood relationship in a straight line upward or downward and collateral line up to the third degree, or who has a marital relationship, or a person who is a dependent of women and/or children;
22. Community is individuals, families, groups, and social organizations and/or community organizations.
23. Child-Friendly Regency is a Regency that has a child rights-based development system through the integration of commitments and resources of government, community, business and media that are planned comprehensively and sustainably in policies, programs and activities to ensure the fulfillment of children's rights and special protection of children.
24. Private Sector is Non-Governmental Institutions, Business World and Mass Media.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

Protection and empowerment of women and children shall be conducted based on the principles of:
a. Humanity;
b. Justice and gender equality;
c. Shelter;
d. Best interests of the victim;
e. Survival of the mother;
f. Survival and development of the child;
g. Non-discrimination;
h. Confidentiality of the victim;
i. Anti-violence;
j. Empowerment of the victim;
k. Holistic.

Article 3

The objectives of protection and empowerment of women and children are to:
a. Fulfill children's rights;
b. Prevent acts of violence against women and children including trafficking in persons;
c. Eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
d. Protect and provide a sense of security for women and children;
e. Provide services to women and child victims of violence;
f. Protect children from child marriage and mediate domestic disputes to realize harmonious and prosperous household integrity.

CHAPTER III

CHILD-FRIENDLY REGENCY

Article 4

1. In the context of implementing the Convention on the Rights of the Child and various laws and regulations and policies related to the fulfillment of children's rights and special protection of children, the Regional Government, private sector and community institutions make efforts to realize a Child-Friendly Regency.
2. To realize a Child-Friendly Regency as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government, private sector and Community Institutions shall pay attention to children's rights related to the right to life, development, protection and participation.
3. The Regional Government, Private Sector and Community Institutions in realizing a Child-Friendly Regency are incorporated in the Child-Friendly Regency Task Force.
4. The Child-Friendly Regency Task Force is responsible for the fulfillment of children's rights related to:
a. Civil rights and freedoms;
b. Family environment and alternative care;
c. Basic health and welfare;
d. Education, leisure time utilization and cultural arts activities;
e. Special protection of children.

CHAPTER IV

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN FROM ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 5

The rights of victims and their families to obtain Facilities in the service process consist of/include:
a. Right to health recovery and right to psychological recovery from suffering experienced by victims;
b. Right to legal assistance;
c. Right to obtain information;
d. Right to social recovery;
e. Right to restitution (trafficking, rape);
f. Right to spiritual services;
g. Right to reintegration;
h. Right to shelter services;
i. Right to an advocate.

Article 6

Child victims of acts of violence, in addition to obtaining the rights as referred to in Article 5, also obtain special rights, as follows:
a. Right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;
b. Right to basic services;
c. Right to equal protection;
d. Right to be free from various stigma; and
e. Right to obtain freedom.

CHAPTER V

OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

Article 7

Obligations and responsibilities in providing protection and Empowerment for women and children are the joint responsibility of:
a. Government/Regional Government and Government Institutions;
b. Private Sector and Community Institutions;
c. Society;
d. Family and or Parents.

Article 8

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 7 letter a, include:
a. Establishing policies for protection, empowerment of women and children;
b. Implementing policies, protection, empowerment of women and children from acts of violence established by the government;
c. Conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children;
d. Providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children;
e. Allocating budget for the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children, according to regional financial capacity;
f. Fostering and supervising the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children; and
g. Realizing a child-friendly regency.
2. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent establishes programs and action activities for the protection and Empowerment of women and children in the Regional Action Plan as a basis for SKPD in implementing the protection and Empowerment of women and children.
3. The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), is part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan (RPJMD).
4. Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), are regulated by Regent Regulation.

CHAPTER VI

ROLE OF GOVERNMENT, PRIVATE SECTOR AND COMMUNITY INSTITUTIONS

Article 11

1. To prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government conducts empowerment and awareness to families, parents, and society by providing information, guidance and/or counseling and realizing a Child-Friendly Regency.
2. In addition to empowerment and awareness as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government conducts efforts as follows:
a. Increasing the number and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. Providing accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. Providing employment for women;
d. Building community participation and concern for the prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. Building and providing an information system that is easily accessible;
f. Building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women and children;
g. Optimizing the integrated service center for women and children empowerment (P2TP2A) or by another name as a post for protection of women and children from acts of violence; and
h. Opening complaint posts for the protection of women and children based on community.

Article 12

1. Prevention of the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 11, is implemented by SKPD whose duties and functions are in the field of:
a. Social;
b. Health;
c. Education;
d. Manpower;
e. Women's empowerment and child protection;
f. Mental and spiritual; and
g. peace and order.
3. Prevention of acts of violence by SKPD as referred to in paragraph (1), is implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

CHAPTER VII

SERVICES FOR VICTIMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 13

1. Forms of services provided to women and children victims of acts of violence, are as follows:
a. Complaint services;
b. Health services;
c. Legal services;
d. Repatriation;
e. Recovery, social reintegration, and medicolegal;
f. Identification services;
g. Psychological services; and
h. Shelter.
2. Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), are implemented according to minimum service standards established by the Government and implemented by SKPD whose duties and functions are in the field of:
a. Social;
b. Economy;
c. Health;
d. Women's empowerment and child protection; and
e. Mental and spiritual.
3. In carrying out the duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government cooperates with Government Agencies, other Regional Governments, and society.
4. Further provisions regarding the procedures for handling services for women and children from acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2) and paragraph (3), are regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 17

In addition to establishing the Integrated Service Center for Women and Children Empowerment (P2TP2A) or other designation as referred to in Article 16, in order to support the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence, the Regent shall establish:
a. Task force on trafficking in persons;
b. Task force on prevention of child marriage;
c. Regional action committee on the elimination of the worst forms of child labor;
d. Child-friendly regency task force or other designation.

Article 19

1. In order to achieve the objectives of protection of women and children, cooperation may be carried out with:
a. Central government;
b. Provincial Government/other Provinces;
c. Regency/City Government;
d. Non-governmental institutions; and
e. Community.
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. Exchange of data and information;
b. Recovery of victims of acts of violence;
c. Repatriation and social reintegration;
d. Provision of evidence and witnesses; and
e. Followed up in accordance with applicable law.
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be set forth in the form of a Joint Agreement.
